{"answerArea":{"calculator":false,"chi2Table":false,"periodicTable":false,"tTable":false,"zTable":false},"hints":[{"content":"First, we note that $r=-\\dfrac65$ makes the denominator zero and so cannot be a solution.\n\nNow, we multiply both sides of the equation by $5r+6$ to get rid of the quotient. ","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"$\\begin{align}\n\\dfrac{4r}{5r + 6} \u0026= \\dfrac{1}{6}\n\\\\\\\\\n(5r+6)\\cdot \\dfrac{4r}{5r + 6} \u0026= (5r+6)\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{6}\n\\\\\\\\\n4r\u0026=\\dfrac56 r+1\n\\\\\\\\\n\\dfrac{19}{6}r\u0026=1\n\\\\\\\\\nr\u0026=\\dfrac{6}{19}\n\\end{align}$","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"We get that $r=\\dfrac{6}{19}$ is a potential solution. It doesn't make the denominator zero, so it is indeed a solution.\n\n**In conclusion, $r=\\dfrac{6}{19}$.**","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}}],"itemDataVersion":{"major":0,"minor":1},"question":{"content":"**Solve for $r$.**\n\n$\\dfrac{4r}{5r + 6} = \\dfrac{1}{6}$\n\n$r =$ [[☃ numeric-input 1]]","images":{},"widgets":{"numeric-input 1":{"alignment":"default","graded":true,"options":{"answers":[{"answerForms":["proper","improper"],"maxError":0,"message":"","simplify":"required","status":"correct","strict":false,"value":0.3157894736842105}],"coefficient":false,"labelText":"","multipleNumberInput":false,"size":"normal","static":false},"static":false,"type":"numeric-input","version":{"major":0,"minor":0}}}}}
